WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.700
Transnational organized crime
represents an acute threat to the United

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.889
States and to our neighbors in the
Western Hemisphere. In Mexico, for example,

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:17.850
drug cartels are taking advantage of
globalization in order to move their

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.500
product across borders, and to launder the assets through our own financial system.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.630
They represent a serious threat to the
communities of Mexico, to our border, and

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.439
to, frankly, our international financial
system. There's a narco-state in

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:36.570
Venezuela that's gradually destroyed the
institutions in that country and

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:40.920
represents the threat to Venezuela's
neighbors, and a conduit for drug

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.700
trafficking to the United States. In
Colombia, we have narco guerrillas that

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.289
operate with virtual impunity over
decades, and now threaten the ability of

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:53.370
people there in Colombia to make peace.
The United States can be a partner with

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:58.320
all of our neighbors to tackle this
problem, use some asymmetrical tools to

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.910
freeze their assets, but also build up
the capacity of these countries to

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.900
confront this problem in their own
national territory.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.220
How do you think the United States should work with its
neighbors to confront transnational

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.729
organized crime? Let us know in your
comments, and let us know what other

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:20.090
topics you'd like a AEI scholars to
cover in 60 seconds.

